    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. what about liquid assets like bank accounts or stocks? do those get treated differently if the deceased was a resident of the uk but the accounts are held in a spanish bank?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Liquid assets in a Spanish bank are generally considered foreign assets for UK Inheritance Tax (IHT) purposes because they are located outside the UK. Since you are a UK resident, the UK tax authorities will typically claim jurisdiction over your worldwide estate, including those foreign funds, regardless of where the physical accounts are held. This means an asset might not be included in the Spanish inheritance tax calculation if the total taxable worldwide estate already exceeds the Spanish exemption threshold, potentially avoiding double taxation on that specific item. You must ensure you declare these foreign assets to the HM Revenue & Customs to avoid penalties, which usually prevents the need to pay both taxes on the identical value of those funds.",
      "source": "target",
